WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.195
Crack a bullwhip, and a tiny flick of
the wrist becomes a huge snap at the tip.

2
00:00:05.325 --> 00:00:08.199
Supply chains can do the exact same thing.

3
00:00:09.549 --> 00:00:14.285
The bullwhip effect is the tendency
for supply chain managers at different

4
00:00:14.285 --> 00:00:18.381
levels to exaggerate the need to
increase or decrease inventory,

5
00:00:18.381 --> 00:00:22.087
when customer demand changes
or becomes unpredictable.

6
00:00:23.437 --> 00:00:25.479
Let's follow one kayak season.

7
00:00:25.609 --> 00:00:28.395
Shoppers buy ten percent
more kayaks one week.

8
00:00:28.395 --> 00:00:29.614
Barely a ripple.

9
00:00:29.744 --> 00:00:33.706
The retailer worries about running
out, so it orders twenty percent more.

10
00:00:33.836 --> 00:00:37.402
The wholesaler sees that jump,
and orders forty percent more,

11
00:00:37.402 --> 00:00:38.602
just to be safe.

12
00:00:38.717 --> 00:00:41.298
By the time the signal
reaches the manufacturer,

13
00:00:41.298 --> 00:00:42.893
it's cranking out double.

14
00:00:44.243 --> 00:00:46.285
Then demand settles back down.

15
00:00:46.415 --> 00:00:48.984
Now everyone is stuck
with extra inventory,

16
00:00:48.984 --> 00:00:50.249
and orders crash.

17
00:00:50.379 --> 00:00:54.147
Remember the empty shelves
and panic buying of 2020?

18
00:00:54.147 --> 00:00:55.919
Same whip. Real life.

19
00:00:57.269 --> 00:00:59.013
The cure is visibility.

20
00:00:59.143 --> 00:01:01.761
When every level sees real consumer sales,

21
00:01:01.761 --> 00:01:05.938
through vendor-managed inventory
or shared systems like blockchain,

22
00:01:05.938 --> 00:01:07.435
nobody has to guess.

23
00:01:07.565 --> 00:01:10.351
Less guessing means smaller
swings, lower costs,

24
00:01:10.351 --> 00:01:11.762
and fewer stockouts.

25
00:01:13.112 --> 00:01:15.654
Now try the simulator. Crank up the panic,

26
00:01:15.654 --> 00:01:19.293
then turn on data sharing,
and watch the whip calm down.

